= BARK: a successor to the nbtree index access method =

''Status:'' design, in progress. This document is commit 0001 of the BARK
patch series and is the contract every later commit refers to. Update it in
the same commit that changes a contract; do not let code and this document
drift.

== What BARK is ==

BARK is a new index access method (<code>CREATE INDEX ... USING bark</code>),
intended over time to be a drop-in successor to <code>nbtree</code>: it keeps
every nbtree capability (ordered scans, uniqueness, multicolumn keys, INCLUDE
columns, deduplication, index-only scans, parallel build and scan, the
amcheck contract) and adds capabilities the B-tree shape cannot express
cheaply:

* '''Arbitrary-size keys and values.''' nbtree caps a key at roughly a third
  of a page and stores no payload beyond the heap TID (and INCLUDE columns).
  BARK stores keys and values whose size is bounded by the index, not by the
  page, in the tradition of Berkeley DB.
* '''Sorted duplicates.''' A key may map to many values held in sorted order,
  again in the libdb tradition, rather than to a single heap pointer plus a
  deduplicated TID posting list.
* '''An inverted / posting-list paradigm''' as a first-class shape, so an
  entry's "value" side can be a set of row locators. This is the GIN/RUM
  paradigm brought under one AM (it is what OrioleDB's and DocumentDB's
  inverted indexes need), rather than a separate AM per shape.
* '''Multithread-readiness.''' No process-global mutable state on the read or
  write path; every long-lived pointer is annotated for the multithreaded
  PostgreSQL work (see the <code>xtc</code> worktree's
  <code>utils/global_lifetime.h</code>).

BARK is '''not''' a replacement for the catalog and TOAST indexes. Those stay
on nbtree by deliberate policy (see [[#The capability sweep|the capability
sweep]]); BARK targets user indexes.

=== Relationship to the sbm ADT (commits 0001-ADT / 0002) ===

The posting-list ("value is a set of locators") paradigm rests on a compact,
sorted, mergeable integer-set representation. That is the '''sbm''' ADT
(sparse bitmap set, <code>src/backend/lib/sbm.c</code> +
<code>src/include/lib/sbm.h</code>), vendored from the sparsemap library and
committed ahead of the AM as a standalone, independently tested helper (model:
<code>lib/integerset</code>, not a <code>Node</code> type).

sbm's measured envelope drives where the posting-list shape pays off:

* As a raw TID posting list of uniformly random TIDs it costs about 24 bytes
  per TID -- ~4x worse than the raw 6-byte ItemPointer -- so BARK '''must not'''
  encode a small or scattered dup set as an sbm.
* For a '''clustered / low-cardinality''' dup set (the case an inverted index
  actually produces: a hot trigram, a common attribute value) it costs
  0.2-1.9 bytes per TID and its set-algebra (union/intersect for
  BitmapAnd/BitmapOr) runs in O(chunks), not O(TIDs).

So the AM chooses the encoding per posting list by density, and sbm is the
substrate for the clustered case only. sbm's build path is O(N log N) through
<code>sbm_add_many_grow</code> (sort + threaded cursor); a naive per-element
random insert loop is O(N^2) and BARK must route bulk posting-list
construction through the bulk entry point.

== The six re-federation contracts ==

nbtree is not "just an AM": twenty-odd places in the backend assume the only
ordered index is a B-tree and the only row identifier is a heap TID. Before
BARK can be a peer of nbtree, those assumptions have to be named as contracts
that either side can declare and the dependent code can test. Five of the six
contracts already exist as landed work in sibling worktrees and are
cherry-picked in; the sixth is the sweep that removes the hardcodes. KNN
(<code>amcanorderbyop</code>) is reserved in the API from day one and
implemented late (phase P07).

=== C-LOCATOR: what an index entry points at ===

''Source: <code>tepid</code> @ <code>64fcb891</code>
(<code>src/include/access/amlocator.h</code>).''

An index entry stores a '''locator''': the value it hands the table AM to
identify a row. Today every table AM hands out an <code>ItemPointerData</code>
and every index AM stores one, so the contract holds by convention and is
never checked. Correctness of bitmap scans, index-scan prefetch, and TID-range
scans each rests on a specific property of that locator.

The contract names those properties as a closed
<code>LocatorCapability</code> enum (<code>LOCATOR_CAP_TID = 0</code> is the
heap's ItemPointer) and a <code>LocatorCapabilityInfo</code> describing each:

* <code>size</code> -- fixed byte size, or 0 for variable length;
* <code>decomposable</code> -- splits into (page, in-page slot), the
  precondition for bitmap scans and BitmapAnd/BitmapOr;
* <code>prefetchable</code> -- the page component is a real table block, so
  index-scan prefetch may feed it to the read stream;
* <code>stable</code> -- the locator identifies the same logical row for that
  row's whole lifetime (heap: false; in-place-update AM: true);
* <code>old_version_is_materialized</code> -- the pre-update image stays
  fetchable through its own locator until end of command (heap: true;
  in-place AM: false -- must capture the old image before overwriting);
* the "may be inexact" property that pairs with C-MVCC's recheck.

'''AM-routine wiring:''' <code>TableAmRoutine.locator_capability</code>
(the one capability a table provides) and
<code>IndexAmRoutine.amcanlocators</code> (a bitmask of the capabilities an
index can store, built with <code>LOCATOR_CAP_MASK()</code>).
<code>DefineIndex</code> checks
<code>amRoutine->amcanlocators & LOCATOR_CAP_MASK(tablecap)</code> and rejects
an index that cannot store the table's locator.

'''BARK obligation:''' declare <code>amcanlocators</code> including
<code>LOCATOR_CAP_TID</code>; lay entries out using
<code>GetLocatorCapability(cap)->size</code>; never assume 6-byte TIDs in the
on-disk format.

=== C-MVCC: visibility, SSI, and the index-only / recheck path ===

BARK entries obey the same visibility rules as nbtree. Three obligations:

* '''Snapshot visibility and SSI.''' Scans acquire the same predicate locks
  and honor the same snapshot semantics as nbtree; a serialization-relevant
  read takes a predicate lock at the granularity nbtree would.
* '''Index-only scans need a leaf-key recheck that deduplicates.''' Re-running
  <code>indexqualorig</code> against the live tuple drops false positives but
  '''cannot''' dedup two index entries that chain-walk to the same live tuple
  (that returns duplicate rows), and per-scan TID dedup breaks
  <code>ORDER BY</code> (it yields the tuple at the stale key's sort
  position). The correct general mechanism is comparing the '''stored leaf
  key''': BARK must retain enough of the leaf key to do this.
* '''The recheck-hint bit.''' ''Source: <code>tepid</code> @
  <code>236cff2f</code> (<code>src/include/storage/itemptr.h</code>).'' A
  locator whose match is not exact carries
  <code>ItemPointerRecheckHintFlag</code> (bit 14 of the offset;
  <code>ItemPointerHasRecheckHint</code> / <code>ItemPointerSetRecheckHint</code>,
  masked off by <code>ItemPointerOffsetNumberMask</code>). BARK sets it on any
  entry that requires a heap recheck, and <code>tbm_add_tuples</code> already
  consumes it to force a page lossy.

=== C-DELETE: delete-marking via UNDO ===

''Source: undo branch v11 (<code>src/include/access/amapi.h</code>,
<code>access/nbtree.h</code>, <code>access/tableam.h</code>).''

An AM over an UNDO-backed table participates in index delete-marking rather
than leaving dead entries for VACUUM:

* <code>TableAmRoutine.am_supports_undo</code> gates the whole mechanism;
  <code>RelationUsesIndexUndo()</code> (the <code>index_undo</code> reloption)
  is the per-relation switch.
* <code>IndexAmRoutine.amdeletemark</code> / <code>amundomark</code> (both may
  be NULL for an AM that opts out) mark and un-mark an entry.
* nbtree's encoding, which BARK mirrors: an alternate-TID subtype with
  <code>BT_IS_DELETE_MARKED = 0x4000</code> set (and <code>BT_IS_POSTING</code>
  clear) in the entry's <code>t_tid</code> offset. <code>genam.h</code>'s
  <code>index_delete_mark</code> / <code>index_undo_mark</code> dispatch to the
  AM hooks; the UNDO rmgr replays them.

'''BARK obligation:''' reserve a delete-marked entry subtype in the on-disk
format from the start, and implement <code>amdeletemark</code>/<code>amundomark</code>
when the UNDO integration phase lands (not in the first functional AM).

=== C-KEYSHAPE: the key/value shape and the inverted paradigm ===

This is the contract that is native to BARK rather than inherited. It defines
the entry as a (key, value-set) pair where:

* the key is variable length, ordered by a B-tree-family operator class;
* the value side is one of: a single locator (the nbtree shape), a sorted
  list of locators (sorted duplicates, the libdb shape), or a locator '''set'''
  encoded as a posting list (the inverted shape, sbm-backed when clustered);
* the opclass declares which shapes it supports, so a plain scalar index and
  an inverted index share one AM and one on-disk framework.

This is the contract that lets a future OrioleDB- or DocumentDB-style inverted
index be a BARK opclass instead of a separate AM. The encoding decision
(single / list / posting-set) is per entry and driven by cardinality, using
sbm only where its density envelope wins.

=== C-STATS: statistics and cost ===

BARK feeds the planner the same statistics contract as nbtree:
<code>amcostestimate</code> produces comparable correlation, per-tuple, and
startup costs; <code>ANALYZE</code> sees index expressions the same way; the
planner's ordered-path generation (see the sweep below) treats a BARK scalar
index as interchangeable with a B-tree for costing.

=== The capability sweep ===

There are currently '''30''' <code>BTREE_AM_OID</code> references in the tree.
They fall into three groups; only the middle group is BARK work.

# '''Must stay nbtree (policy).''' Catalog and TOAST index creation
  (<code>catalog/toasting.c</code>), <code>pg_amcheck</code>
  (<code>src/bin/pg_amcheck</code>), and the opclass/opfamily lookup helpers
  that answer "what is the btree opclass for this type"
  (<code>typcache.c</code>, <code>lsyscache.c</code>, <code>typecmds.c</code>,
  <code>opclasscmds.c</code>, <code>amvalidate.c</code>,
  <code>nbtvalidate.c</code>, <code>parse_clause.c</code>). These legitimately
  mean the B-tree operator family, not "the only ordered AM," and are left
  alone.
# '''The sweep target: "btree is the only thing that can produce ordered
  paths / be sorted on / infer an ON CONFLICT arbiter."''' The planner and
  executor sites -- <code>optimizer/path/indxpath.c</code>,
  <code>optimizer/util/plancat.c</code>,
  <code>utils/sort/tuplesortvariants.c</code> (the
  <code>Assert(relam == BTREE_AM_OID)</code>), <code>commands/repack.c</code>,
  <code>utils/adt/ri_triggers.c</code>, <code>commands/tablecmds.c</code> --
  are rewritten to ask an AM capability ("can produce ordered scans",
  "supports this sort") rather than to compare against
  <code>BTREE_AM_OID</code>. This is the largest mechanical part of the series.
# '''Ambiguous, decided case by case''' (<code>access/index/amapi.c</code>'s
  index-AM validation): keep the btree-specific validation for btree, add
  BARK's own.

KNN ordering (<code>amcanorderbyop</code>) is a reserved field in BARK's
<code>IndexAmRoutine</code> from the first commit but is not implemented until
phase P07, so the sweep only has to make ordered ''sort'' paths capability-
driven now, not ordered ''operator'' paths.

== Roadmap ==

Reconstructed phase plan. Each entry is one or a few bisectable commits; every
commit builds under <code>-Werror --enable-cassert</code> and passes
<code>make check</code> on its own (regenerate the initdb template --
<code>trash tmp_install testrun; meson test --suite setup</code> -- before the
regress step so a stale template cannot produce a false diff). Contracts above
are cited by name in each phase's commit messages.

'''Done'''

* '''0001''' -- this design document (the contract of record).
* '''sbm ADT''' -- vendored sparse bitmap set, PG-native, standalone-tested.
  ''(committed)''
* '''test_sbm''' -- the sbm regression + differential-test module, plus the
  sbm-vs-Bitmapset-vs-TIDBitmap benchmark. ''(committed)''

'''F-series -- foundations (make a BARK peer of nbtree structurally)'''

* '''F01''' -- vendor the substrate. ''(done as the sbm ADT above.)''
* '''F02''' -- cherry-pick / rebase the C-LOCATOR contract
  (<code>amlocator.h</code>, <code>amcanlocators</code>,
  <code>locator_capability</code>, the <code>DefineIndex</code> check) from
  <code>tepid</code>.
* '''F03''' -- cherry-pick the recheck-hint bit
  (<code>ItemPointerRecheckHintFlag</code>) from <code>tepid</code>.
* '''F04''' -- the capability sweep, group 2: make ordered-''sort'' path
  generation, <code>tuplesort</code> variants, <code>repack</code>, and the RI
  trigger ask an AM capability instead of comparing to
  <code>BTREE_AM_OID</code>. No BARK code yet; nbtree still answers "yes" to
  every capability, so this is behavior-preserving.
* '''F05''' -- register the empty <code>bark</code> AM: catalog rows,
  <code>IndexAmRoutine</code> skeleton (all capability flags declared,
  including reserved <code>amcanorderbyop</code>), <code>amvalidate</code>,
  build/scan hooks that error "not implemented". <code>CREATE INDEX USING
  bark</code> is accepted and validated but cannot yet be built.
* '''F06''' -- the on-disk page format: meta/root/leaf/internal page layout,
  the C-KEYSHAPE entry encoding (single / sorted-list / posting-set), and the
  reserved delete-marked subtype for C-DELETE. Documented as a companion
  <code>README</code> under the AM directory.

'''A-series -- the working access method'''

* '''A01-A03''' -- insert and split (single-locator shape first): leaf insert,
  page split, high-key / right-link maintenance in the Lehman & Yao tradition
  nbtree uses.
* '''A04-A06''' -- search and ordered scan; the C-MVCC leaf-key recheck and
  index-only-scan support; SSI predicate locking at nbtree granularity.
* '''A07-A09''' -- uniqueness, multicolumn keys, INCLUDE columns.
* '''A10-A12''' -- the sorted-duplicates and posting-set shapes (C-KEYSHAPE),
  sbm-backed for clustered value sets; BitmapAnd/BitmapOr via sbm set-algebra.
* '''A13-A15''' -- deletion, VACUUM, page recycling; then C-DELETE
  delete-marking (<code>amdeletemark</code>/<code>amundomark</code>) once the
  table is UNDO-backed.
* '''A16-A18''' -- parallel build, parallel scan, and the amcheck contract for
  BARK.

'''P-series -- parity and beyond'''

* '''P01-P03''' -- performance parity with nbtree on the scalar workload
  (build throughput, scan latency, space) using the benchmark harness pattern
  from test_sbm; A/B against <code>master</code>'s nbtree.
* '''P04-P06''' -- arbitrary-size keys/values beyond the nbtree page cap; the
  C-STATS cost model tuned to the new shapes.
* '''P07''' -- KNN / <code>amcanorderbyop</code>: implement ordered-''operator''
  scans and extend the capability sweep to ordered-operator path generation.

== Ground rules ==

* This document is authoritative for the contracts. A commit that changes a
  contract updates this file in the same commit.
* Catalog and TOAST indexes stay on nbtree; BARK is for user indexes.
* No process-global mutable state on the BARK read/write path; annotate
  long-lived pointers per the <code>xtc</code> multithreading work.
* Every commit is independently buildable and testable (bisectable), builds
  <code>-Werror --enable-cassert</code>, and passes <code>make check</code>.
